{%- comment -%}
Adapted from /home/rmdes/zachleat.com/_includes/post-list-entry.html. That
source is title-centric (year subheads with sparklines, popularity ranking,
og-image previews) built for a single post shape that always has a title.
None of that applies here: four of Indiekit's six post types (note, bookmark,
like, reply) are titleless by IndieWeb design and are the majority of what
gets published, not an edge case — a listing built around headlines would
silently drop or blank them (the same mistake that produced empty <title>
elements in the Atom feed earlier in this project, see eleventyComputed.js).

So this partial does NOT reimplement per-type rendering: it dispatches to the
same _includes/types/*.liquid partials entry.liquid already uses (Task 6),
via the identical {% case postType %} switch, so every list entry renders
exactly what its own permalink page renders — title for article, content for
note, the target link for bookmark/like/reply, the image for photo. The only
genuinely new thing here is the <li> wrapper.

The permalink <a class="u-url"> itself is NOT rendered by this partial — it
was originally placed here, as a sibling *before* the <article
class="h-entry"> each type partial emits, which put it outside the h-entry
entirely. A real mf2 parser confirmed the consequence: all 7 permalinks
collapsed onto the h-feed root's own `url` property and every h-entry child
had none, defeating the entire point of publishing an h-feed (microsub
readers/webmention senders/aggregators have nothing to link back to). Fixed
by moving the anchor INTO each _includes/types/*.liquid partial, immediately
inside its own <article class="h-entry"> (article.liquid wraps its existing
h1 title in it instead of adding a second visible link; the other five had
no title text on display at all, so they gained one). Type partials are
shared with the standalone permalink page (via layouts/entry.liquid), so the
anchor's href is `page.url`, not `post.url` — `page` is already correctly
scoped to the post being rendered (see the `page: post` override discussed
below), so it resolves to the right permalink from both call sites with no
new parameter needed. This also means standalone permalink pages now carry a
`u-url` on their own h-entry for the first time — they had none before
either.

The link text is `title`, likewise already in scope inside the type
partials because LiquidJS `{% include %}` shares the caller's scope — not
conditionally branched per type — because _data/eleventyComputed.js already
computes a non-empty title for every post (a real title for article/bookmark,
the bookmarked URL for a titleless bookmark, "<Type> · <date>" for
note/like/reply/photo). Using it here gives every entry a meaningful, unique
label for free and matches what @11ty/eleventy-plugin-rss already puts in the
Atom feed's <title> for the same post — one fallback, reused, not a second
one invented for the listing.

Expects a local `post` (one `collections.post` item) AND `page` overridden to
that same `post` — both passed by the caller (content/index.liquid). The
`page` override matters: isoInstant/humanDate (eleventy.config.js) re-read
the *current* page's own front matter via `this.page.inputPath` to detect an
explicit UTC offset. Looped straight from the homepage with no override,
`this.page` would stay bound to content/index.liquid (no `date:` line at
all), every post would be mis-detected as zone-less, and each already-correct
instant would be re-anchored by the configured timezone's offset — wrong, but
not obviously so (still a plausible-looking ISO string). Passing `page: post`
on the include (LiquidJS locals shadow the parent scope for that render only)
makes the filters read each post's own file, exactly as they do when that
page renders itself.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign date = post.data.date -%}
{%- assign title = post.data.title -%}
{%- assign content = post.templateContent -%}
{%- assign bookmarkOf = post.data.bookmarkOf -%}
{%- assign likeOf = post.data.likeOf -%}
{%- assign inReplyTo = post.data.inReplyTo -%}
{%- assign photo = post.data.photo -%}
{%- assign postType = post.data.postType -%}
<li>
	{% case postType %}
		{% when "article" %}{% include "types/article.liquid" %}
		{% when "bookmark" %}{% include "types/bookmark.liquid" %}
		{% when "like" %}{% include "types/like.liquid" %}
		{% when "photo" %}{% include "types/photo.liquid" %}
		{% when "reply" %}{% include "types/reply.liquid" %}
		{% else %}{% include "types/note.liquid" %}
	{% endcase %}
</li>
